From 309a34cbb12e0dd70eccd957c7153b8e9c55cd1c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 16:35:59 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Write the RT-11 REL module from an ELF link

The last emulation still reading a.out.  Most of the writer did not
care what the object format was -- it works through
bfd_canonicalize_symtab/reloc and bfd_get_section_contents -- but two
things did.

The relocated words were empty.  a.out keeps a relocation's addend in
the word itself, so the writer could read the value straight out of the
section content; ELF keeps it in a field of its own and leaves the word
alone.  Nobody else was going to fill those in: of the RLD entry types
this writer emits, a loader patches only the direct ones and treats a
displaced (PC-relative) entry as already correct, which it can, since a
displacement inside one module does not depend on where the module
lands.  So rel_apply_relocs now writes every word itself, as the module
would look loaded at 0 with the p-sects laid end to end -- which is
exactly how it is loaded, see layout() in this project's libppu.  That
also makes explicit what a.out only ever arrived at by accident, and
the p-sect bases the writer needs are now stated rather than taken from
whatever addresses the linker happened to give the sections.

References to globals defined in the same module had to be resolved
here.  a.out's `-r` link reduced them to section-relative relocations by
itself; ELF keeps them against the symbol, and the loader on the other
end cannot resolve anything by name -- libppu's ppuc_rel.c rejects the
Global entry types outright.  rel_classify_symbol therefore asks which
p-sect a symbol's *section* is, rather than whether it is a section
symbol, and a reference to a defined global comes out as the p-sect
entry the loader can use.

With that, the emulation moves to TEMPLATE_NAME=elf like the other two,
which lets it drop its copy of pdp11.em's option handling and script
selection.

Checked against the module the a.out toolchain builds from the same
source, examples/gfour's PPU side, compared block by block: every TXT
block identical -- the code and data of the module are the same bytes --
and the GSD entries identical as a set.  Two differences, both expected:
the order the global symbols come out of the symbol table, which is the
object format's business, and one extra displaced RLD entry for the
same-module global reference a.out had folded away, which the loader
skips.

Then run: the ELF-built .ppu loaded into the PPU by the a.out-built
gfour.sav draws gfour's screen correctly on the emulated machine.
---
 ld/emulparams/pdp11rt11rel.sh |  21 +--
 ld/emultempl/pdp11rt11rel.em  | 302 ++++++++++++++--------------------
 2 files changed, 137 insertions(+), 186 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ld/emulparams/pdp11rt11rel.sh b/ld/emulparams/pdp11rt11rel.sh
index 823bdbc0ffd..4948c26fbed 100644
--- a/ld/emulparams/pdp11rt11rel.sh
+++ b/ld/emulparams/pdp11rt11rel.sh
@@ -2,22 +2,23 @@
 # `-m pdp11rt11rel`: produce a relocatable RT-11 object module (the
 # native DEC "Relocatable Object Language" format documented in the
 # RT-11 Volume and File Formats Manual -- GSD/TXT/RLD/ENDMOD blocks,
-# RADIX-50 names) instead of an a.out-pdp11 object.
+# RADIX-50 names) instead of an ordinary object.
 #
-# Like pdp11rt11sav, OUTPUT_FORMAT is deliberately "a.out-pdp11", not a
-# format of its own: linking goes through a.out-pdp11's own specialized,
-# proven-correct relocatable-link path (pdp11_aout_link_input_section in
-# bfd/pdp11.c, in its `if (relocatable)` branch -- this emulation is
-# meant to be used with `-r`), producing exactly the file
-# `ld -r -m pdp11rt11` would.  Once that file is fully written to disk,
+# Like pdp11rt11sav, OUTPUT_FORMAT is deliberately the ordinary object
+# format and not one of its own: linking goes through that backend's own
+# relocatable-link path (this emulation is meant to be used with `-r`),
+# producing exactly the file `ld -r -m pdp11rt11` would.  Once that file
+# is fully written to disk,
 # emultempl/pdp11rt11rel.em's after_close_output hook reads its symbols,
-# relocations and section contents back with ordinary a.out-pdp11 BFD
-# calls and repacks them into the RT-11 object format in place.  See the
+# relocations and section contents back with ordinary BFD calls and
+# repacks them into the RT-11 object format in place.  See the
 # comment on gld${EMULATION_NAME}_after_close_output in that file for
 # the full story.
 SCRIPT_NAME=pdp11rt11
-OUTPUT_FORMAT="a.out-pdp11"
+TEMPLATE_NAME=elf
+OUTPUT_FORMAT="elf32-pdp11"
 TEXT_START_ADDR=512
 EXTRA_EM_FILE=pdp11rt11rel
 ARCH=pdp11
 ENTRY=start
+EMBEDDED=yes
diff --git a/ld/emultempl/pdp11rt11rel.em b/ld/emultempl/pdp11rt11rel.em
index a17233ddc11..016c0ed1012 100644
--- a/ld/emultempl/pdp11rt11rel.em
+++ b/ld/emultempl/pdp11rt11rel.em
@@ -18,136 +18,18 @@
 # Foundation, Inc., 51 Franklin Street - Fifth Floor, Boston,
 # MA 02110-1301, USA.
 #
-# This is pdp11.em (same PDP11-Unix-compatible --omagic/--imagic option
-# handling as the plain pdp11/pdp11rt11 emulations) plus one extra hook:
-# after_close_output, which converts a `-r` link's a.out-pdp11 output
-# into the native RT-11 relocatable object format ("REL": GSD/TXT/RLD/
-# ENDMOD blocks -- see the big comment on that function below).
+# An EXTRA_EM_FILE on top of emultempl/elf.em: everything an ELF
+# emulation does, plus one hook, after_close_output, and the REL
+# writer it calls.  (It used to carry a copy of emultempl/pdp11.em's
+# option handling and script selection as well, because the emulation
+# was built on the generic template rather than the ELF one; elf.em
+# does all of that.)
 
